#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" :question/math-academy-id "e-2711" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" :question/worked-solution "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B$. We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n73^{∘} + m∠B &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 180^{∘} - 73^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 107^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n41^{∘} + 107^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n148^{∘} + 2x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 32^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 16^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation\n\n$$\nm\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) = m∠A + m∠C\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]) &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\n73^{∘} &= 41^{∘} + 2x \\\\\n32^{∘} &= 2x \\\\\n16^{∘} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba344d2f-aeb4-54ca-ab5d-e0abeceddba5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "d9bf29fd-5151-591f-b6af-8f5e53b3d231" :question/math-academy-id "e-2712" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6e/6eb087290fa6ea66325d35fa626ef385bb4d4e477e6e1c40057b9f9d7e43d9d4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the exterior angles of a triangle sum to $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n145^{∘} + x + (2x - 25^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n120^{∘} + 3x &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 240^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66f215f1-b90c-5284-8628-6a94bf37de0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Interior Angle Given the Exterior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "e97d1ab9-f0b2-5e27-afff-a84da69c7487" :question/math-academy-id "e-2714" :question/problem "Given a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, let the exterior angles at ${A}$ and ${B}$ be equal $120^\\circ$ and $100^\\circ$, respectively. What is the measure of the angle $\\angle C$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by drawing the corresponding triangle.\n\n![](images/3e/3e2698fe8e2e886127669b1e04f45499706750312b447ff0c73524f2de4ff2ce.png)\n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at ${C}$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is $360^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120^{∘} + 100^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n220^{∘} + z &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nz &= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠C &= 180^{∘} - z \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 140^{∘} \\\\\n&= 40^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle C = 40^\\circ\n$$"}]}
